WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:01.960
- [Instructor] In this
video we're going to talk

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:04.000
about one of the most important ideas

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.950
in mathematics and that's
whether order matters

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:09.440
when you multiply two numbers.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:13.720
So for example, is three times four

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:17.360
the same thing as four times three?

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:21.300
Are these two things equal to each other?

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.390
And regardless of whether
these two are equal

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:25.200
to each other, is it always the case

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:26.640
that if I have some number times some

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.530
other number, if I swap
the order am I going

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:32.510
to get an equivalent number?

00:00:32.510 --> 00:00:35.260
Well, pause this video and see
if you can work through that.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:36.540
Try to think about that a little bit

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:38.340
before we think through it together.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:41.780
Well, let's think through
this particular example.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:46.210
And we're going to do so with
the help of some angry cats.

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:48.793
So we clearly see some angry cats here.

00:00:49.701 --> 00:00:51.150
Meow.

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:52.890
Yes, they are angry.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:56.320
We could view this as
three groups of four.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:59.990
So this is one group right over here

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:02.700
of angry cats, four angry cats.

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:05.990
This is two groups of four angry cats.

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:09.890
And this is three groups
of four angry cats

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:12.920
if we view the first number here as groups

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:14.580
of the second number.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:17.990
But we could also view it
as four groups of three.

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:19.810
How would we do that?

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:24.810
Well, we could have one
group of three angry cats.

00:01:24.910 --> 00:01:29.000
We can have two groups
of three angry cats.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:32.490
We can have three groups
of three angry cats.

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:37.210
And we could of course have
four groups of three angry cats.

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:39.200
So based on that, if you think of it,

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:42.290
the first number as groups
of the second number,

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:44.750
well, it seems like the
order doesn't matter.

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:46.010
Another way you could think about it

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:49.970
is here you have four
rows of three angry cats.

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:54.720
You have one, two, three, four rows

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:58.500
of one, two, three angry cats.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:00.620
And so to figure out how many total cats

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:04.540
you have, you multiply four times three.

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:08.220
But you could view this
same group of angry cats

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:11.720
but just view it with a
slightly different perspective.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:14.830
So here we have our angry cats.

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:18.190
And then let me rotate our angry cats,

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:21.560
probably risking making
them a little bit angrier.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:22.993
Let me move them back in.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:28.250
And now, we could view this as three rows,

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:31.900
one, two, three, of four cats in each row.

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:33.910
So let me put all of these upright.

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:38.180
So we have one, two,
three rows of one, two,

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:39.900
three, four cats.

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:41.290
Don't wanna look at those 'cause it might

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:43.350
make us a little bit confused.

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:47.100
And we're dealing with the
exact same number of cats.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:49.890
And so I'm only dealing
with three and four here

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:52.580
but what you will see
is order doesn't matter

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:54.800
when you are multiplying two numbers.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:57.800
And we could also see
that on a number line.

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:01.050
And we could do that
with multiple examples.

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:02.950
I'll keep a couple of angry cats here

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:05.423
looking at us just to keep us in check.

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:08.420
If we wanna think about three times four

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:11.150
we could view it as four threes.

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:15.967
So three, six, nine, 12.

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:19.890
Or we could view it as three fours.

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:23.980
Four, eight, and 12.

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:25.700
And I focused a lot on three and four

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:27.470
but we could do it with any two numbers

00:03:27.470 --> 00:03:29.460
that we're trying to multiply together.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:33.230
So let's say we wanted to do that with,

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:37.610
I don't know, let's see
whether six times four,

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:41.560
whether it's the same
thing as four times six.

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:44.050
Well, you could view it as six fours.

00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:49.050
Four, eight, 12, 16, 20, and then 24.

00:03:54.470 --> 00:03:58.180
Or you could view it as four sixes.

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:03.180
One six, 12, 18, and 24.

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:08.120
So big takeaway, order doesn't matter

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:10.010
when you are multiplying
numbers like this.

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:11.190
And this is sometimes referred

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:13.590
to as the commutative property.

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:16.120
It's a fancy word but
it's really just saying

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:18.170
that whether you're doing six times four

00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:20.760
or four times six the commutative property

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:23.077
of multiplication says,
"Hey, those two things

00:04:23.077 --> 00:04:25.247
"are going to be equivalent."

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:28.210
Meow.

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:30.423
Yes, they are angry for being rotated.

